<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qaitbay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1416–<a href="/years/1496">1496</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Al-Ashraf Qaitbay</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qaitbay was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> from 1468 to 1496, one of the longest and most stable reigns of the Circassian period. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> to a standstill in a war of 1485–1491 and was a great builder, leaving his funerary complex in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>'s Northern Cemetery and the Citadel of Qaitbay at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. Heavy military spending and plague strained his treasury.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qaitbay.jpg" alt="Qaitbay" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Florentine painter Cristofano dell'Altissimo, 16th century · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mamluk_Sultan_Kayitbay_by_Florentine_painter_Cristofano_dell%27Altissimo_Galleria_degli_Uffizi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
